WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.540
Take a look at the world today.

00:02.120 --> 00:04.340
Last year, the average attacker went from getting

00:04.340 --> 00:06.180
in to moving through a network in just 29

00:06.180 --> 00:08.760
minutes, and the fastest did it in 27

00:08.760 --> 00:09.660
seconds.

00:10.020 --> 00:13.520
Yet it still takes an organisation 241 days to

00:13.520 --> 00:14.740
find and contain a breach.

00:15.520 --> 00:18.500
More than 48,000 new vulnerabilities were published in

00:18.500 --> 00:20.700
a single year, and there are nearly 5

00:20.700 --> 00:23.360
million security jobs that nobody can fill.

00:23.800 --> 00:25.560
You simply can't hire your way out of

00:25.560 --> 00:26.460
that.

00:26.520 --> 00:29.380
That's why we built TRShell, a terminal with

00:29.380 --> 00:30.280
an agent in it.

00:30.680 --> 00:33.040
Instead of helping with one command and then

00:33.040 --> 00:35.580
waiting on you, it runs the whole engagement

00:35.580 --> 00:39.000
from scope and recon to evidence and the

00:39.000 --> 00:41.500
report with every command out in the open.

00:42.480 --> 00:44.380
It starts with a plan and you choose

00:44.380 --> 00:45.740
the model for every role.

00:46.360 --> 00:49.020
It works right inside your real terminal and

00:49.020 --> 00:50.660
it can drive a real browser too.

00:51.300 --> 00:53.520
When the job gets big, it hands pieces

00:53.520 --> 00:56.600
to subagents, each on its own limits, and

00:56.600 --> 00:59.060
it warns you before anything steps outside your

00:59.060 --> 00:59.960
scope.

00:59.980 --> 01:02.240
If a command fails, it works out why

01:02.240 --> 01:03.140
and tries again.

01:03.760 --> 01:05.960
Whatever it learns about a target, it keeps

01:05.960 --> 01:08.880
for next time. And every finding gets written

01:08.880 --> 01:11.200
up with a message to your phone the

01:11.200 --> 01:12.100
moment it's done.

01:13.000 --> 01:14.780
And it gets sharper with every engagement.

01:15.540 --> 01:18.460
Confirmed findings go into an engagement ledger, so

01:18.460 --> 01:20.280
even if you delete the session, the work

01:20.280 --> 01:21.180
survives.

01:21.480 --> 01:23.080
When it discovers a new way to test,

01:23.080 --> 01:25.900
it proposes it for your checklist, but nothing

01:25.900 --> 01:27.540
is written until you approve it.

01:29.580 --> 01:32.980
With 58 built-in tools, any MCP server,

01:33.440 --> 01:36.020
and fully local models through Ollama, it fits

01:36.020 --> 01:37.800
right into the way you already work.

01:39.020 --> 01:42.000
TRShell, a terminal with an agent in it.
